<Base locale={locale} title="Where to Stay — Marienbad.com">
  <PillarPage locale={locale} title="Where to Stay" description="Find your perfect stay — from historic spa hotels to cosy guesthouses.">

    <h2>A Town of Grand Hotels</h2>
    <p>
      Mariánské Lázně was built for guests. From its very beginnings in the early nineteenth century, the town developed hand-in-hand with its hotels, and many of the grand buildings that define Marienbad's skyline were designed as places to stay, to heal and to be pampered. Today, that tradition continues: Marienbad offers accommodation ranging from palatial spa hotels with century-old pedigrees to friendly family-run pensions, modern apartments and budget-friendly guesthouses.
    </p>
    <p>
      Whatever your style and budget, the key to a great Marienbad stay is choosing the right location and the right type of accommodation for your plans — whether that means a full therapeutic spa programme, a weekend of golf and relaxation, or a base for exploring the forests and castles of western Bohemia.
    </p>

    <h2>Luxury Spa Hotels</h2>
    <p>
      Marienbad's top-tier hotels are among the finest spa properties in Central Europe. Housed in magnificently restored nineteenth-century buildings, they combine period architecture — sweeping staircases, chandeliers, ballrooms — with modern wellness infrastructure, including treatment centres, thermal pools, saunas and medical consultation rooms.
    </p>
    <p>
      Ensana Health Spa Hotels operates several of the town's most iconic properties, including the Nové Lázně (New Spa) — home to the only Roman bath in the Czech Republic — and the Centrální Lázně (Central Spa), directly connected to the Main Colonnade. These hotels offer comprehensive spa packages that include medical consultations, personalised treatment plans, mineral baths, peat wraps and drinking cures, alongside fine dining and elegant common areas.
    </p>
    <p>
      What distinguishes a luxury spa stay in Marienbad from a conventional hotel experience is the integration of wellness into every aspect of the visit. The day has its own rhythm: a morning visit to the springs, treatments before lunch, a walk in the park, afternoon rest, an evening concert or dinner. Guests often describe the experience as transformative — not because of any single treatment, but because the entire environment is designed to promote healing and calm.
    </p>

    <h3>What to Expect at a Top Spa Hotel</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Initial medical consultation</strong> — A spa physician assesses your health and designs a personalised programme.</li>
      <li><strong>Daily treatments</strong> — Typically three to five treatments per day, which might include mineral baths, peat wraps, massages, electrotherapy or inhalations.</li>
      <li><strong>Full or half board</strong> — Most spa packages include meals, often with menus designed by nutritionists to complement the treatment programme.</li>
      <li><strong>Wellness facilities</strong> — Indoor pools, whirlpools, saunas, steam rooms, fitness centres and relaxation lounges.</li>
      <li><strong>Cultural programmes</strong> — Many hotels organise concerts, lectures and excursions for their guests.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Mid-Range Hotels</h2>
    <p>
      Marienbad has an excellent selection of mid-range hotels, many of which occupy handsome historic buildings and offer their own spa and wellness facilities. While smaller and less opulent than the grand spa hotels, these properties often provide outstanding value: comfortable rooms, on-site restaurants, swimming pools or saunas, and access to basic spa treatments such as massages and mineral baths.
    </p>
    <p>
      Several mid-range hotels are located along Hlavní třída (the main boulevard) and in the streets immediately surrounding the spa district, placing you within easy walking distance of the colonnades, parks and springs. This category is ideal for visitors who want the convenience of a hotel with some spa amenities but prefer to explore the town's restaurants and attractions independently.
    </p>

    <h2>Pensions and Guesthouses</h2>
    <p>
      For a more intimate and often more affordable stay, Marienbad's pensions and guesthouses are an excellent choice. Typically family-run, these properties offer clean, comfortable rooms with breakfast included, and the personal attention of hosts who know the town inside out. Many pension owners are happy to recommend restaurants, arrange excursions or share local tips that you won't find in any guidebook.
    </p>
    <p>
      Pensions are scattered throughout the town, from the quiet residential streets above the spa district to the more lively areas near the main boulevard. Some are located in beautifully restored villas with gardens, offering a genuinely homely atmosphere.
    </p>

    <h2>Apartments and Self-Catering</h2>
    <p>
      An increasing number of visitors — particularly families and those planning longer stays — choose self-catering apartments. These range from modern studio flats to spacious multi-room apartments in period buildings, and they offer the freedom to set your own schedule, cook your own meals (the local farmers' market is a delight) and live, however briefly, like a Marienbad local.
    </p>
    <p>
      Apartments are available through major booking platforms as well as local agencies. Many are centrally located and represent excellent value, especially for stays of a week or more.
    </p>

    <h2>Best Areas to Stay</h2>

    <h3>The Spa District (Centre)</h3>
    <p>
      The obvious choice for first-time visitors. Staying in the spa district puts you steps from the Main Colonnade, the Singing Fountain, the Cross Spring and the central parks. This is where you will find the majority of the grand spa hotels, as well as mid-range options and several pensions. The area is largely pedestrianised and beautifully maintained.
    </p>

    <h3>Hlavní třída and Surroundings</h3>
    <p>
      The main boulevard running through town is lined with shops, restaurants and cafes. Hotels and pensions here are slightly less expensive than those directly in the spa district but are still within a five-minute walk of all major attractions. The atmosphere is a touch more lively and urban.
    </p>

    <h3>The Hillside Neighbourhoods</h3>
    <p>
      For those who prefer quiet and greenery, the residential streets above the spa district offer pensions and apartments with garden settings and, in some cases, lovely views over the town. The trade-off is a slightly longer walk (often uphill on the return) to the centre, but many visitors consider the tranquillity well worth it.
    </p>

    <h2>Booking Tips</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Book early for summer.</strong> July and August are peak months, and the best spa hotels and apartments fill up well in advance — often three to six months ahead.</li>
      <li><strong>Consider the shoulder seasons.</strong> May, June, September and October offer pleasant weather, fewer crowds and often lower prices. Autumn in Marienbad is particularly beautiful.</li>
      <li><strong>Spa packages save money.</strong> If you plan to have treatments, booking a spa package through your hotel is almost always more economical than paying for treatments individually.</li>
      <li><strong>Check what is included.</strong> Spa hotel rates often include meals and treatments. Compare the total value, not just the room rate.</li>
      <li><strong>Ask about minimum stays.</strong> Some spa hotels, particularly for medical programmes, have a minimum stay of five to seven nights. Shorter wellness breaks are usually available as well.</li>
      <li><strong>Parking is usually available.</strong> Most hotels and many pensions offer free or inexpensive parking, which is convenient if you are arriving by car or plan day trips.</li>
    </ul>

    <blockquote>
      <p>"In Marienbad, the hotel is not merely a place to sleep — it is the frame within which the entire spa experience unfolds. Choose well, and the town will work its quiet magic."</p>
    </blockquote>

    <h2>Our Recommendation</h2>
    <p>
      For the most complete Marienbad experience, we suggest staying at least five nights in a property that offers spa treatments on site. This gives you enough time to settle into the town's gentle rhythm, follow a short treatment programme, explore the colonnades and parks, take a day trip to Karlovy Vary or Loket Castle, and — most importantly — slow down. Marienbad rewards those who give it time.
    </p>

